1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,500
Hi! This is Seoul's '성수동' [seong-su-dong],

2
00:00:02,500 --> 00:00:06,826
full of red-brick buildings and lovely cafes.

3
00:00:06,826 --> 00:00:07,403
Today,

4
00:00:07,403 --> 00:00:15,190
let's learn to describe a person's '외모' [oe-mo] (looks) and '성격' [seong-gyeok] (personality) in Korean!

5
00:00:18,070 --> 00:00:21,848
When we introduce a friend or describe someone,

6
00:00:21,848 --> 00:00:25,545
we say how they look — '생기다' [saeng-gi-da] — and their '성격' [seong-gyeok].

7
00:00:25,545 --> 00:00:28,680
Just two things: looks and personality!

8
00:00:34,620 --> 00:00:40,692
We describe people mostly with '형용사' [hyeong-yong-sa] (adjectives) — words for qualities.

9
00:00:40,692 --> 00:00:44,233
Let's meet today's expressions one by one,

10
00:00:44,233 --> 00:00:45,920
walking through 성수동.

11
00:00:47,570 --> 00:00:52,311
First, height. If someone is tall: '키가 크다' [ki-ga-keu-da].

12
00:00:52,311 --> 00:00:55,950
That person walking by — '키가 커요' [ki-ga-keo-yo].

13
00:00:59,180 --> 00:01:03,263
The opposite: '키가 작다' [ki-ga-jak-da] (short).

14
00:01:03,263 --> 00:01:07,618
'작다' [jak-da] is the adjective for small.

15
00:01:07,618 --> 00:01:10,340
A child is '키가 작아요' [ki-ga-jak-a-yo].

16
00:01:13,930 --> 00:01:18,388
Now hairstyle. Long hair: '머리가 길다' [meo-ri-ga-gil-da].

17
00:01:18,388 --> 00:01:22,210
That cafe owner has '머리가 길어요' [meo-ri-ga-gil-eo-yo].

18
00:01:26,280 --> 00:01:32,757
Short hair: '머리가 짧다' [meo-ri-ga-jjal-da]. '짧다' [jjal-da] is for short length.

19
00:01:32,757 --> 00:01:36,340
The barista has '머리가 짧아요' [meo-ri-ga-jjal-a-yo].

20
00:01:39,930 --> 00:01:44,127
If something is lovely to look at: '예쁘다' [ye-ppeu-da] (pretty).

21
00:01:44,127 --> 00:01:47,065
Use it for people and things alike.

22
00:01:47,065 --> 00:01:50,590
This flower — and that person — are '예뻐요' [ye-ppeo-yo].

23
00:01:54,320 --> 00:01:57,624
For a handsome face: '잘생기다' [jal-saeng-gi-da].

24
00:01:57,624 --> 00:02:02,580
Often used for men, but not only. '잘생겼어요' [jal-saeng-gyeot-eo-yo]!

25
00:02:06,240 --> 00:02:10,173
성수동 is full of stylish people, right?

26
00:02:10,173 --> 00:02:14,318
For someone chic and impressive: '멋있다' [meot-it-da].

27
00:02:14,318 --> 00:02:17,400
That outfit is really '멋있어요' [meot-it-eo-yo].

28
00:02:20,990 --> 00:02:23,691
Slim and trim: '날씬하다' [nal-ssin-ha-da].

29
00:02:23,691 --> 00:02:28,480
And if someone wears glasses: '안경을 쓰다' [an-gyeong-eul-sseu-da].

30
00:02:33,970 --> 00:02:36,030
Now personality.

31
00:02:36,030 --> 00:02:41,439
Warm and helpful to others: '친절하다' [chin-jeol-ha-da] (kind).

32
00:02:41,439 --> 00:02:46,590
The cafe staff in 성수동 are really '친절해요' [chin-jeol-hae-yo].

33
00:02:47,040 --> 00:02:51,786
Kind-hearted and helpful: '착하다' [chak-ha-da].

34
00:02:51,786 --> 00:02:58,904
Similar to '친절하다' [chin-jeol-ha-da], but '착하다' [chak-ha-da] is about the heart.

35
00:02:58,904 --> 00:03:01,870
That child is '착해요' [chak-hae-yo].

36
00:03:05,540 --> 00:03:09,963
Fun to be around and funny: '재미있다' [jae-mi-it-da].

37
00:03:09,963 --> 00:03:16,030
The opposite is '재미없다' [jae-mi-eop-da]. My friend is so '재미있어요' [jae-mi-it-eo-yo].

38
00:03:21,130 --> 00:03:24,870
Quiet and calm, few words: '조용하다' [jo-yong-ha-da].

39
00:03:24,870 --> 00:03:27,180
The opposite of loud.

40
00:03:27,180 --> 00:03:30,590
That customer is always '조용해요' [jo-yong-hae-yo].

41
00:03:33,510 --> 00:03:38,463
Bright and full of energy: '활발하다' [hwal-bal-ha-da] (lively).

42
00:03:38,463 --> 00:03:40,997
The opposite of quiet.

43
00:03:40,998 --> 00:03:45,490
The kids running around 성수동 are '활발해요' [hwal-bal-hae-yo].

44
00:03:47,020 --> 00:03:50,734
Bright and clever: '똑똑하다' [ttok-ttok-ha-da] (smart).

45
00:03:50,734 --> 00:03:53,902
Understands anything quickly.

46
00:03:53,902 --> 00:03:56,960
That student is very '똑똑해요' [ttok-ttok-hae-yo].

47
00:03:59,760 --> 00:04:05,760
To join two traits in one sentence, use '-고' [--go].

48
00:04:05,760 --> 00:04:12,027
Add '-고' [--go] after an adjective and it means 'and'.

49
00:04:12,027 --> 00:04:14,160
'크다' [keu-da] + 고 = '크고' [keu-go].

50
00:04:20,080 --> 00:04:22,406
Let's connect them.

51
00:04:22,406 --> 00:04:29,507
'키가 크다' [ki-ga-keu-da] + '친절하다' [chin-jeol-ha-da] with '-고' [--go] → '키가 크고 친절해요' [ki-ga-keu-go-chin-jeol-hae-yo] — tall and kind.

52
00:04:29,507 --> 00:04:33,180
Looks first, then personality.

53
00:04:39,150 --> 00:04:40,143
One more.

54
00:04:40,143 --> 00:04:46,208
'머리가 길다' [meo-ri-ga-gil-da] + '예쁘다' [ye-ppeu-da] → '머리가 길고 예뻐요' [meo-ri-ga-gil-go-ye-ppeo-yo] — long hair and pretty.

55
00:04:46,208 --> 00:04:50,620
That describes the cafe owner perfectly.

56
00:04:53,950 --> 00:04:57,828
You can join two personalities too.

57
00:04:57,828 --> 00:05:03,036
'재미있다' [jae-mi-it-da] + '활발하다' [hwal-bal-ha-da] → '재미있고 활발해요' [jae-mi-it-go-hwal-bal-hae-yo] — fun and lively.

58
00:05:03,036 --> 00:05:04,920
That's my friend!

59
00:05:08,730 --> 00:05:13,555
A cafe in 성수동 has all kinds of people.

60
00:05:13,555 --> 00:05:19,015
One is '키가 작고 조용해요' [ki-ga-jak-go-jo-yong-hae-yo]. Another is '멋있고 똑똑해요' [meot-it-go-ttok-ttok-hae-yo].

61
00:05:19,015 --> 00:05:22,190
Just link them like that.

62
00:05:22,670 --> 00:05:29,599
Let's put it together to introduce a friend: '제 친구는 머리가 길고 친절해요.

63
00:05:29,599 --> 00:05:32,630
그리고 아주 재미있어요. ' Now you try!

64
00:05:34,040 --> 00:05:36,235
Quick recap.

65
00:05:36,235 --> 00:05:43,553
Looks — '키가 크다' [ki-ga-keu-da], '머리가 짧다' [meo-ri-ga-jjal-da], '예쁘다' [ye-ppeu-da], '멋있다' [meot-it-da].

66
00:05:43,553 --> 00:05:50,321
Personality — '친절하다' [chin-jeol-ha-da], '조용하다' [jo-yong-ha-da], '똑똑하다' [ttok-ttok-ha-da].

67
00:05:50,321 --> 00:05:53,980
Then link with '-고' [--go].

68
00:05:57,630 --> 00:06:05,591
Now walk the 성수동 alleys and describe people in your head: '저 사람은 키가 크고 멋있어요.

69
00:06:05,591 --> 00:06:08,210
' Practice becomes skill.

70
00:06:08,660 --> 00:06:09,884
Great job today!

71
00:06:09,884 --> 00:06:12,790
Now you can describe people in Korean.

72
00:06:12,790 --> 00:06:16,155
See you next time with more fun expressions.

73
00:06:16,155 --> 00:06:16,920
'안녕히 가세요' [an-nyeong-hi-ga-se-yo]!

